WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.207
This query first creates a CTE named older patients.

00:00:04.207 --> 00:00:08.727
That stage contains one row per patient older than fifty.

00:00:08.727 --> 00:00:13.917
The final query left joins admissions and counts admissions per older patient.

00:00:13.917 --> 00:00:17.297
Run it and identify the grain of each stage.

00:00:17.297 --> 00:00:23.392
The CTE is patient grain, and the final grouped result is also one row per patient.

00:00:23.392 --> 00:00:28.232
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:28.232 --> 00:00:33.997
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:33.997 --> 00:00:35.962
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:35.962 --> 00:00:43.402
In a real setting such as a sales team calculating running revenue and top products by region,

00:00:43.402 --> 00:00:49.362
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:49.362 --> 00:00:52.257
before discussing syntax.

00:00:52.257 --> 00:00:58.468
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
